    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Baleen whales (Mysticeti) are gigantic filter feeders that exploit large numbers of small-bodied prey suspended in the ocean using a keratinized oral filter called baleen. Although the gross anatomy of baleen has been described for more than a century, the detailed three-dimensional morphology of baleen plates remains unknown. Using CT scans of baleen from a fin whale (Balaenoptera physalus) and a sei whale (Balaenoptera borealis), I quantified the three-dimensional anatomy of baleen plates in order to calculate the area available for flow during filtration. From the flow area for a standardized section of baleen belonging to each species, I calculated what percentage of the total area was taken up by plate material; the remaining area was considered potentially available for flow. Analyses of the flow area through a section of 37 plates indicate that the maximum areas available for flow in the fin whale can be found closer to the lingual edge (17.9% of the way across the baleen rack) than in the sei whale (69.2%). Given the relative size of the whales, the fin whale, a much larger animal, exhibited a greater peak area of 0.08413m^2 at 0.035m from the lingual edge than the peak area of 0.0095m^2 measured 0.045m from the lingual edge in the sei whale. Furthermore, after analyzing three sections (anterior, middle, and posterior) along a sei whale baleen rack, the greatest total area was found in the posterior section at 0.0198m^2.These data provide the first three-dimensional measures of baleen morphology using computed tomography and significantly increase our understanding of this poorly understood yet critical filtering structure.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
